"Heraclitus of Ephesus was a contemporary of the Buddha, and his surviving sayings state the doctrine in nearly the same image. You cannot step twice into the same rivers; for fresh waters are ever flowing in upon you. And of the one who steps: We step and do not step into the same rivers; we are and are not. He saw, as the Buddha did, that the person changes along with the water. He also held that change has an order. Fire turns to sea and sea to earth in fixed measures, and the measure, which he called the logos, is the same for all and does not change. A river keeps its identity through the flowing. So Heraclitus has a constant after all, the law of change itself. The Buddha said something similar of dependent origination, that it holds whether or not anyone discovers it. The two part on what the insight is for. Heraclitus wanted his hearers to understand the world. He offers no path and promises no release.": "04f25bf13c9b",
"Heraclitus said you cannot step into the same river twice. The Buddha added that the one stepping in is not the same either.": "558e5c4fcd99",
"Impermanence is why change for the better is possible. A mind that could not change could not be trained.": "3c97a27a4729",
"No one in a Western audience will dispute that everything changes. They have heard it from Heraclitus, from the Stoics now sold in airport bookshops, and from every greeting card. Agreement comes too easily, and the teaching slides off. Two things help. The first is to move from the general statement to something happening now: the sound in the room that has already ended, the breath that has just gone out, the mood they walked in with. The second is to say early that the point is practical and hopeful. Many will have heard impermanence as a counsel of sadness, or as a reason to seize the day. Say that the Buddha put it in the same sentence as an instruction to keep working, and ask why he would do that.": "854001210f96",
"On the conclusion Heraclitus is closest. He and the Buddha agree that everything in the world of experience is in flux and that the one who experiences is in flux as well. On the purpose Marcus Aurelius is closest. He alone among the three treats the thought of change as something to be practiced, daily, for the sake of freedom from attachment. Whitehead supplies the most developed theory and the least practice.": "801329856076",
"The Buddhas final instruction to his monks, as the tradition records it, was a single line: Conditions fall apart. Persist with diligence. He had said the same to Ānanda, who was grieving at the news that his teacher would soon die. Did I not prepare for this when I explained that we must be parted and separated from all we hold dear and beloved? How could it possibly be so that what is born, created, conditioned, and liable to wear out should not wear out, even the Realized Ones body? The teaching is stated here without softening and applied to the speaker himself. Whatever is put together by conditions comes apart when the conditions change. This holds for mountains and for moods, and it holds for the body of a Buddha.": "bf97aafa158d",
"The Buddhas last words were: Conditions fall apart. Persist with diligence. Both halves matter.": "adecea92bc01",
"The Stoics remind themselves that everything passes. Buddhists train to watch it pass.": "32e4f905288c",
"The discourses return to the point with images drawn from water. Standing by the Ganges, the Buddha asked his monks to consider a lump of foam carried along by the river. A person who looked closely would find it completely vacuous, hollow, and insubstantial. The body is like that, he said, and he went on through the rest of what a person is: Form is like a lump of foam; feeling is like a bubble; perception seems like a mirage; choices like a banana plant; and consciousness like an illusion. Of an ancient teacher he reported the saying that human life is like a drop of dew on a grass tip. When the sun comes up it vanishes right away. The images are aimed at something specific. They describe the five things we take ourselves to be.": "eebcb149db42",
"The emperor Marcus Aurelius wrote notes to himself in the evenings while commanding an army on the Danube, and a large part of them is a meditation on impermanence. Time is like a river made up of the events which happen, and a violent stream; for as soon as a thing has been seen, it is carried away, and another comes in its place, and this will be carried away too. He gives himself instructions: Observe constantly that all things take place by change. Loss is nothing else than change. The universe is transformation: life is opinion. This is the nearest thing in the West to the Buddhist practice. The thought is repeated daily in order to weaken attachment to fame, comfort, and life. It differs in what it leans on. Marcus believed that the changes are governed by a rational and benevolent nature, so that whatever happens is for the good of the whole, and that his own reason is a fragment of that nature which he can keep upright. His calm comes from trusting the river.": "e5f358228c15",
"The first further step has been stated: the Buddha lets the river run through the one who stands on the bank. Western philosophy from Plato to Descartes looked for the part of us that does not change and identified it with what we really are. The Buddha looked in the same place and reported that awareness arises with its object and ceases with it, like a monkey moving through the forest, which grabs hold of one branch, lets it go, and grabs another. Whether he was right can be tested by observation, and the tradition supplies the method for making it.": "ca11d3568090",
"The likeness ends at the same point in each case: each of the three keeps something out of the river. Heraclitus keeps the logos. Marcus keeps the ruling reason within him and the providence without. Whitehead keeps Gods memory. The Buddha keeps nothing of that kind. He applies impermanence to the mind that observes it, and that is why his version leads to not-self and theirs do not. There is also a difference in how the insight is supposed to work. For the Stoic it is a thought to be remembered, a correct opinion that steadies the mind. For the Buddhist it is something to be watched in the arising and passing of each breath and feeling, until the mind stops reaching for what it has seen dissolve. Marcus tells himself that all is change. The Buddha asks his monks to look.": "312c217925a7",
"The pain comes less from the change than from our demand that things stay as they are.": "a82754eba7a1",
"The second step is one of tone, and Western listeners most often miss it. The last words have two halves. Conditions fall apart is followed at once by Persist with diligence, and the second half depends on the first. If things did not change, a deluded mind would stay deluded, grief would not pass, and no training could have any effect. Impermanence is the reason that the path is possible. Later teachers pressed the point. The Chinese monk Sengzhao argued that because each moment is complete in its own place, nothing is carried away, and the Japanese teacher Dōgen went as far as to say that impermanence is itself Buddha-nature. The early teaching is plainer. What can arise can cease, and that includes ignorance and craving. Heraclitus, Marcus, and Whitehead each found a way to be reconciled to the river. The Buddha taught his students to cross it, and held that it can be crossed because it flows.": "6db45c528441",
"We all know that things change. The practice is to notice it while it is happening.": "58b30570595c",
"What makes the teaching more than an observation is what follows from it. Impermanence is the first of three marks, and the other two are derived from it. All conditions are impermanent—when this is seen with wisdom, one grows disillusioned with suffering: this is the path to purity. What is impermanent cannot give lasting satisfaction, so it is unsatisfactory. What is impermanent and unsatisfactory cannot rightly be called mine or myself. The Buddha also said what the insight does when it is cultivated. The perception of impermanence, developed, eliminates all ignorance and eradicates all conceit I am, as a farmers plow cuts through all the roots in a field. The claim, then, is that seeing change clearly, in oneself, loosens the hold of everything that depends on not seeing it.": "7820719d09eb",
"Whitehead thought that Western philosophy since Plato had taken the wrong side. It had treated permanence as the mark of what is real and change as a lesser condition. He proposed the reverse. The world consists of events that arise, reach completion, and perish, and what we call an enduring thing is a pattern repeated across a series of them. Perpetual perishing, a phrase he took from Locke, was for him the basic character of time. This matches the Buddhist analysis closely, as the previous lesson noted. Whitehead felt the loss in it and looked for a remedy. In his system each event, having perished, is taken up and preserved in the experience of God, so that nothing is finally lost. The Buddha offered no such preservation. On his account the wish that things be kept is the trouble, and the remedy is to see through the wish.": "15ee9908a95f"
}
